{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"PMCID:PMC3371090","internalQuery":"PMCID:PMC3371090","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":2,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"22484291","source":"MED","pmid":"22484291","pmcid":"PMC3371090","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC3371090"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.healun.2012.02.033","title":"High cumulative dose exposure to voriconazole is associated with cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma in lung transplant recipients.","authorString":"Singer JP, Boker A, Metchnikoff C, Binstock M, Boettger R, Golden JA, Glidden DV, Arron ST.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Singer JP","lastName":"Singer","firstName":"Jonathan P","initials":"JP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0224-7472"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, University of California at San Francisco, San Francisco, California 94115, USA. arrons@derm.ucsf.edu"}]}},{"fullName":"Boker A","lastName":"Boker","firstName":"Andreas","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Metchnikoff C","lastName":"Metchnikoff","firstName":"Christopher","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Binstock M","lastName":"Binstock","firstName":"Maxwell","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Boettger R","lastName":"Boettger","firstName":"Rebecca","initials":"R"},{"fullName":"Golden JA","lastName":"Golden","firstName":"Jeffrey A","initials":"JA"},{"fullName":"Glidden DV","lastName":"Glidden","firstName":"David V","initials":"DV","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5888-1419"}},{"fullName":"Arron ST","lastName":"Arron","firstName":"Sarah T","initials":"ST","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5669-0627"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5669-0627"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5888-1419"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0224-7472"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"31","journalIssueId":1939369,"dateOfPublication":"2012 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2012,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Heart Lung Transplant","title":"The Journal of heart and lung transplantation : the official publication of the International Society for Heart Transplantation","essn":"1557-3117","issn":"1053-2498","nlmid":"9102703","isoabbreviation":"J Heart Lung Transplant"},"printPublicationDate":"2012-07-01"},"pubYear":"2012","pageInfo":"694-699","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Lung transplant recipients (LTR) have an increased risk of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) due to immunosuppressive therapy. Voriconazole, which is associated with phototoxic side effects in some patients, may be an additional risk factor for SCC in this population.<h4>Methods</h4>To test whether voriconazole is a risk factor for developing SCC in LTR, we evaluated cumulative exposure to voriconazole in 327 adults who underwent lung transplantation at one center between 1991 and 2010. Voriconazole exposure was assessed as a time-varying covariate. We used survival analysis methods to assess the risk of developing SCC over time.<h4>Results</h4>Exposure to voriconazole was associated with a 2.6-fold increased risk for SCC. This phenomenon was dose-dependent: the risk for SCC increased by 5.6% with each 60-day exposure at a standard dose of 200 mg twice daily. At 5 years after transplant, voriconazole conferred an absolute risk increase for SCC of 28%.<h4>Conclusions</h4>These results suggest that caution should be taken when using voriconazole in LTR because this drug increases the already high risk for SCC in this population.","affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, University of California at San Francisco, San Francisco, California 94115, USA. arrons@derm.ucsf.edu","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S.","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","resea